          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In enumerative
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorics"} combinatorics
            = succeed "\u201D:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorification
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a = b
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} natural number
            expressions is proved by taking underlying cardinalities of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection"} bijection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \cong B
            between sets of structures, hence the name. (If taking cardinalities is an example of “decategorifying”, then going the other way from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a = b
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \cong B
            would be “categorifying” or promoting an equation to an isomorphism.)
          %p
            In some sense this is what the theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/species"} species
            is all about: formalizing the operations adhering to bijective proofs. Often bijective proofs give an impression of elegance or enlightenment: rather than prove natural number or polynomial identities through acts of symbolic manipulation, one
            %em sees
            the identity by drawing pictures of structures that the two sides of the identity are counting.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p For the moment we’ll just jot down some possible examples for illustrating the method; these are to be filled in later, or to be linked to other nLab articles where the bijective proof is given.
          %h3#binomial_identity Binomial identity
          %p
            See for now
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binomial+theorem"} binomial theorem
          %h3#binomial_identity_2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            \-binomial identity
          %p
            For more details, see the
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaussian_binomial_coefficient"} Wikipedia article
          %p
            Algebraically, one may introduce the
            = succeed "-binomial" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                        %mi n
                        %mi k
                      %mo )
                    %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \binom{n}{k}_q
            by introducing a
            = succeed "-affine" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+affine+algebra"} quantum affine algebra
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mi q
                  %mo =
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %mi q
                  %mi x
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[x, y]_q = k\langle x, y \rangle/(y x = q x y)
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi q
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q \in k
            is a parameter, and then writing
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mi y
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %msubsup
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %mi n
                    %msub
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                          %mi n
